<section>
<h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>Late in development, I switched from YouTube audio rips to episodes downloaded directly from Libsyn.</p>
<p>The Libsyn files were higher quality, consistently named, and gave me one canonical source per episode instead of a collection of full episodes, clips, shorts, and mirrored uploads spread across YouTube.</p>
<p>Libsyn was intended as a going-forward source change. I ran several older episodes through the pipeline as a spot check.</p>
<p>That is when Episode 136 produced something I did not expect.</p>
<p>Two versions of the same episode produced different transcripts.</p>
<p>Both had gone through substantially the same downstream processing. But a statement by David Friedberg appeared in an older transcript generated from the lower-quality source and disappeared from the newer Libsyn transcription.</p>
<blockquote><p>David Friedberg: “Oh, my god.”</p></blockquote>
<p><p>The omission itself is tiny, but what bothered me was what it implied. I had spent an enormous amount of time building QA around transcript errors after transcription: bad speaker attribution, fragmented sentences, contaminated turns, duplicated text, broken alignment. I had not seriously considered that the transcriber itself might silently delete speech upstream of all of that work. There was no blank line, no <code>[inaudible]</code>, no broken timestamp, no indication that anything was missing. I spent the next several weeks hunting for speech I knew existed, like one of those 1990s paranormal ghost-hunting shows. Hence the name: <strong>Ghost Audio.</strong></p>

<p>Ghost Audio is a deletion in word error rate: speech exists in the source audio but never appears in the resulting transcript. WER normally assumes a reference transcript exists, but Episode 136 had no trusted reference. Producing one was the entire point of this project, and the transcript I was producing was already demonstrating that it could not safely be treated as ground truth. The important issue is therefore not whether one short phrase disappeared. It is <strong>training-data completeness</strong>. A transcript with silent gaps can remove the cause of a later response, merge two ideas that were never adjacent, distort speaker relationships, erase disagreement or acknowledgment, and train a model on conversational transitions that never occurred. The dangerous part is that the transcript can still read perfectly well.</p></p>
</section>
