<p>The first pass therefore stayed entirely on the left side of the pipeline shown above: the source audio itself and the VAD / segmentation stage.</p>

<p><strong>VAD (Voice Activity Detection)</strong> pre-screens audio and passes only likely-speech regions to Whisper. This matters because Whisper tends to hallucinate in silence and low-information segments, inserting unspoken content, often stock YouTube phrases like "thanks" or "like and subscribe." I tested both Pyannote and Silero; both detected speech in the audio windows where Friedberg and Gerstner spoke.</p>

<p><strong>Segmentation:</strong> VAD also has to decide where one chunk of speech starts and ends, and those decisions depend on the audio around it. In other words, <strong>where you cut the audio can have big downstream consequences.</strong> I compared Pyannote and Silero around the same opening passage. Friedberg’s line was present when the Pyannote chunk was passed to reference Whisper, but missing on the slightly different Silero chunk and on the plain 0–30s chunk. Small boundary changes were enough to change what Whisper produced.</p>

<p><strong>Pitch (F0):</strong> Pitch is basically how high or low a voice sounds. I had a hunch that Chamath’s long JCal riff was the dominant sound in this part of the clip and was making Friedberg’s much shorter reaction harder to pick out. That hunch came partly from NeMo diarization, which kept assigning Friedberg’s line to Chamath when the line appeared at all. I used a short Chamath-only section as a subtraction reference, then ran pYIN on what was left. That revealed a pitch trace during Friedberg’s missing “Oh, my god,” which was more evidence that his voice was really there even though the transcript dropped it. Gerstner’s line did not produce the same clear result. Because subtracting audio can create artifacts, I treated this as supporting evidence, not proof.</p>
