<h3>Acoustic Conclusion</h3>
<p>For Friedberg, the omission could not be explained by missing audio.</p>
<p>His speech was present, although weak:</p>
<ul>
<li>VAD detected speech activity.</li>
<li>RMS energy was present.</li>
<li>pYIN recovered a plausible F0 trace after subtraction.</li>
<li>Whisper showed faint but non-zero attention over the region.</li>
</ul>
<p>Gerstner remained much less tractable:</p>
<ul>
<li>energy was present,</li>
<li>but pitch evidence was weaker,</li>
<li>attention was faint,</li>
<li>and no tested decoder configuration naturally recovered his phrase.</li>
</ul>
<p>I therefore dropped Gerstner from the main recovery path and concentrated on Friedberg.</p>
<p>The question became:</p>
<p><strong>Why wasn't the decoder emitting speech that was actually there?</strong></p>
</section>

<section>
<h2 id="triage-cross-attention">Triage: Cross-Attention</h2>

<p>My production pipeline uses a CTranslate2 decoder, a compiled C++ inference engine that doesn't expose the attention weights I wanted to inspect. So I dropped back to the standard PyTorch Whisper decoder, which let me see them directly. It was significantly slower, but it made the graph possible.</p>

<figure class="figure">
  <a
    href="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/ai-transcription-pipeline-cross-attention.png"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    <img
      src="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/ai-transcription-pipeline-cross-attention.png"
      alt="AI Transcription Pipeline highlighting cross-attention analysis"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </a>
</figure>

<p><strong>Cross-attention</strong> is one of the places where Whisper decides which parts of the audio are most relevant while it generates text. In simple terms, I wanted to see whether the decoder was still looking at the parts of the recording where Friedberg and Gerstner spoke, even though their words never appeared in the transcript.</p>

<div class="figure">
  <a
    href="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    <img
      src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png"
      alt="Whisper cross-attention around both Ghost Audio regions"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </a>
  <p class="figure-caption">
    <em>Whisper cross-attention, summed across audio-aligned heads, over a 30-second window. Dashed lines mark the word timings of the two missing “Oh my god” phrases: Ghost 1 (Friedberg, roughly 21.1–21.6 s) and Ghost 2 (Gerstner, roughly 28.0–28.5 s).</em>
  </p>
</div>

<p>Around Friedberg's missing “Oh, my god,” the attention was near zero, with a few faint bumps against an otherwise flat region. Gerstner's region showed more activity, though it sat next to much stronger speech and was harder to separate cleanly.</p>

<p>That does not mean Whisper correctly recognized either phrase. Attention is not a transcript, and a small signal is not proof that the model “understood” the words. What it did tell me was simpler: the decoder was not entirely ignoring those regions. Some connection remained between text generation and the missing speech, which pushed the investigation farther downstream, into how the decoder chooses what to emit and what to skip.</p>

</section>

<section>
<h2 id="triage-decoder-fork">Triage: Decoder Fork</h2>

<p>By this point, the upstream checks were mostly covered: VAD saw speech, RMS showed energy, pYIN recovered Friedberg’s pitch, and cross-attention was low but present.</p>

<p>So I moved downstream to the part that actually decides what text gets emitted.</p>

<figure class="figure">
  <a
    href="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/pww-pipeline-decoder-analysis.png"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    <img
      src="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/pww-pipeline-decoder-analysis.png"
      alt="PWW Pipeline decoder analysis"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </a>
</figure>

<p>I wanted to see the decision tree the decoder was considering. That turned into about a week of learning how beam search works and rebuilding enough of my pipeline to expose those choices.</p>

<p>My production path used WhisperX with CTranslate2. It was fast, but it did not expose the decoder internals I needed. So I built a parallel pipeline using Pyannote, reference OpenAI Whisper in PyTorch, and Wav2Vec2 alignment. I call that pipeline <strong>PWW</strong>.</p>

<p>PWW let me inspect token probabilities and decoder behavior directly.</p>

<p>At timestamp anchor <code>&lt;|18.98|&gt;</code>, hundreds of possible token paths existed.</p>
<p>Two were especially interesting.</p>
<div class="decoder-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">GHOST PATH · GROUND TRUTH</p>
          <h3>"Oh, my god."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Oh</td><td>89.24%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>,</td><td>49.93%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>my</td><td>97.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>god</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>.</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><strong>Joint: 35.78%</strong></p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">WINNER PATH · PRODUCTION OUTPUT</p>
          <h3>"He's not even here."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>He</td><td>45.29%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>'s</td><td>99.94%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>not</td><td>99.96%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>even</td><td>99.98%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>here</td><td>99.78%</td></tr>
