<h3>Sparse Segmentation</h3>
<p>One decode emitted relatively few timestamp boundaries.</p>
<p>A long segment stretched across the Friedberg region.</p>
<p>His short reaction sat inside a much larger span of surrounding speech and never received its own textual segment.</p>
<h3>Dense Segmentation</h3>
<p>Another decode emitted many more timestamp boundaries.</p>
<p>The model created boundaries around Friedberg's reaction.</p>
<p>That gave <code>"Oh, my god."</code> its own decoding opportunity.</p>
<p>The line survived.</p>
<p>So the emerging mechanism was no longer:</p>
<blockquote><p>turn timestamp tokens on</p></blockquote>
<p>or:</p>
<blockquote><p>turn timestamp tokens off</p></blockquote>
<p>It was:</p>
<blockquote><p>timestamp decisions alter segmentation, and segmentation changes which speech gets an opportunity to survive decoding.</p></blockquote>
</section>
<section>
<h2 id="production-changes-the-story">Production Changes the Story</h2>
<p>Up to this point, most of the investigation had happened on short excerpts around the beginning of Episode 136.</p>
<p>The next step was to run the actual production workload:</p>
<p><strong>the full 98-minute Libsyn episode.</strong></p>
<p>That result broke the simpler hypothesis.</p>
<p>Production WhisperX generated 259 segments.</p>
<p>Friedberg's <code>"Oh, my god."</code> was still absent around 20–23 seconds.</p>
<p>Gerstner's was still absent around 27.5–29.5 seconds.</p>
<p>There were several unrelated <code>"oh my god"</code> occurrences elsewhere in the episode, but neither ghost appeared where it belonged.</p>
<p>That immediately raised another possibility:</p>
<p>maybe the full-file VAD segmentation differed from the short excerpt tests.</p>
<p>It did not.</p>
<p>Pyannote produced an opening chunk of approximately:</p>
<p><strong>1.26s–30.98s</strong></p>
<p>That was the same chunk already used in successful reference-Whisper experiments.</p>
<p>So the chunk boundary was not sufficient to explain recovery.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Same Audio, Different Decoder</h2>
<p>I extracted the exact 1.26–30.98s chunk as a NumPy audio array.</p>
<p>Then I sent identical audio into two implementations of Whisper.</p>
<h3>Reference Whisper / PyTorch</h3>
<ul>
<li>Friedberg recovered</li>
<li>dense timestamp segmentation</li>
<li>multiple local segments</li>
</ul>
<h3>WhisperX / CTranslate2</h3>
<ul>
<li>Friedberg dropped</li>
<li>markedly different segmentation behavior</li>
</ul>
<!-- MEDIA SLOT 10: no existing component found -->
<p>The source audio was the same.</p>
<p>The VAD boundary was the same.</p>
<p>The model family was the same.</p>
<p>The decoder implementation was not.</p>
<p>That became one of the strongest findings in the investigation:</p>
<p><strong>the chunk is necessary context, but the decoder implementation can still determine whether the ghost survives.</strong></p>
<p>This also invalidated an earlier assumption that any Whisper implementation should behave roughly the same when fed the same VAD chunk.</p>
<p>It did not.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>A Note on WhisperX Chunk Tests</h2>
<p>One later sweep exposed another trap.</p>
<p>When I fed raw NumPy chunks directly into WhisperX under several decoder configurations, it sometimes returned a single large segment spanning essentially the entire chunk.</p>
<p>Some of those text blobs happened to contain <code>"oh my god."</code></p>
<p>A simple string search would classify that as recovery.</p>
<p>But that behavior did not match the full production pipeline.</p>
<p>So I stopped treating “the words appeared somewhere in a giant chunk transcript” as equivalent to proper recovery.</p>
<p>A recovery test needs to preserve realistic segmentation behavior and survive full-file processing.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 id="two-classes-of-ghost-audio">Two Classes of Ghost Audio</h2>
<p>The investigation started with two people saying the same words seconds apart.</p>
<p>They turned out to represent different failure classes.</p>
<h3>Friedberg: Recoverable</h3>
<p>Once the decoder reaches a favorable state near Friedberg's line, <code>" Oh"</code> can become overwhelmingly likely.</p>
<p>In later token-chain probes it reached roughly <strong>86–88%</strong> under successful trajectories.</p>
<p>Reference PyTorch Whisper can recover the phrase.</p>
<p>The problem is getting the decoder into — and keeping it on — the right trajectory.</p>
<p>Friedberg is therefore primarily a:</p>
<p><strong>path / segmentation / decoder-implementation failure.</strong></p>
<h3>Gerstner: Subthreshold</h3>
<p>Gerstner behaves very differently.</p>
<p>Across forced-prefix and isolation probes, <code>" Oh"</code> remained extremely weak.</p>
<p>Depending on the exact trajectory, later tests placed it around fractions of one percent, sometimes orders of magnitude below the winning token path.</p>
<p>Even when I isolated his time window, the phrase never became strong enough to emerge naturally.</p>
<p>No tested configuration has recovered his <code>"Oh, my god."</code></p>
<p>Gerstner is therefore better described as a:</p>
<p><strong>probability-floor / subthreshold failure.</strong></p>
<p>The distinction matters.</p>
<p>A better beam search or segmentation path can potentially rescue Friedberg.</p>
<p>It cannot rescue words the model never scores competitively in the first place.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 id="hallucination-as-a-forensic-signal">Hallucination as a Forensic Signal</h2>
<p>The PWW pipeline introduced another strange behavior.</p>
<p>At beam=1, reference Whisper sometimes hallucinated repetitive text around the same difficult regions.</p>
