<p>The hallucinations were failures.</p>
<p>But potentially useful failures.</p>
<p>They appeared locally around audio that the decoder was having trouble resolving.</p>
<p>Wav2Vec alignment also failed over several of those hallucinated spans because the generated text did not correspond to actual speech.</p>
<p>That suggested another forensic signal:</p>
<blockquote><p>localized repetition or confabulation over real speech activity may identify a region worth manually reviewing.</p></blockquote>
<p>It does <strong>not</strong> tell me what was actually said.</p>
<p>And it does not recover Gerstner.</p>
<p>But a failure can still be informative if it tells me where to listen.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>Calm-Whisper Probe</h2>
<p>I also tested decoder self-attention head masking based on the Calm-Whisper idea.</p>
<p>The goal was to see whether suppressing known hallucination-prone behavior would expose missing speech underneath it.</p>
<!-- MEDIA SLOT 12: no existing component found -->
<p>The masked decoder:</p>
<ul>
<li>removed the repeated hallucinations,</li>
<li>shortened the decode,</li>
<li>eliminated timestamp emission,</li>
<li>retained one Friedberg <code>"Oh, my god."</code>,</li>
<li>did not recover Gerstner.</li>
</ul>
<p>That was not a production fix.</p>
<p>It did reinforce a broader lesson from the investigation:</p>
<p>decoder interventions can simultaneously affect hallucination, segmentation, and timestamp behavior.</p>
<p>These mechanisms are entangled.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 id="berd-1">BERD-1</h2>
<p>BERD-1 came out of the earlier timestamp experiments.</p>
<p>The original idea was simple:</p>
<p>run two plausible decodes of the same episode and surface places where they disagree.</p>
<p>The first implementation compared:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Timestamp Tokens OFF</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><code>without_timestamps=True</code></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Timestamp Tokens ON</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><code>without_timestamps=False</code></p>
<p>When one transcript contained speech absent from the other, BERD-1 created a candidate for human review.</p>
<!-- MEDIA SLOT 13: no existing component found -->
<p>On Episode 136, this surfaced roughly <strong>5–8 candidates</strong>, depending on settings.</p>
<p>Most were not dramatic missing sentences.</p>
<p>They were short disfluencies:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>uh</code></li>
<li><code>ah</code></li>
<li><code>right</code></li>
</ul>
<p>That is still useful.</p>
<p>Short utterances are exactly the sort of content most likely to disappear without making a transcript look obviously damaged.</p>
<p>But subsequent full-episode testing changed how I interpret BERD-1.</p>
<p>Timestamp configuration did not provide a reliable production recovery of Friedberg.</p>
<p>And isolated WhisperX chunk behavior did not always reproduce the full-file pipeline.</p>
<p>BERD-1 is therefore not a Ghost Audio repair engine.</p>
<p>It is a:</p>
<p><strong>decoder-disagreement detector.</strong></p>
<p>Its job is to say:</p>
<blockquote><p>something about this region is unstable enough that a human should probably listen.</p></blockquote>
<p>That is a much more defensible use of the signal.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2>The Limit of Decoder Disagreement</h2>
<p>Gerstner exposes the fundamental limitation.</p>
<p>A disagreement system can find:</p>
<ul>
<li>decoder A says something,</li>
<li>decoder B says nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>It cannot find:</p>
<ul>
<li>decoder A says nothing,</li>
<li>decoder B says nothing,</li>
<li>but a human hears speech.</li>
</ul>
<p>If no configuration emits the missing text, transcript comparison has nothing to compare.</p>
<p>That is why there is no universal BERD-style solution to Ghost Audio.</p>
<p>Some ghosts leave computational evidence.</p>
<p>Others do not.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 id="what-i-found">What I Found</h2>
<p>The investigation started with a simple question:</p>
<p><strong>Why did one audio source lose three words that another transcript preserved?</strong></p>
<p>The answer became substantially more complicated.</p>
<h4>1. Ghost Audio is real</h4>
<p>Speech can be present in the source recording and silently disappear from an otherwise fluent transcript.</p>
<h4>2. Higher-quality audio does not guarantee a more complete transcript</h4>
<p>The Libsyn source was cleaner than the older YouTube-derived source, yet Friedberg disappeared from the newer transcript.</p>
<h4>3. Friedberg's line is recoverable</h4>
<p>Reference PyTorch Whisper can emit it under favorable decoding conditions.</p>
<h4>4. Gerstner's line is not currently recoverable</h4>
<p>No tested decoder configuration naturally emitted his phrase.</p>
<h4>5. Timestamp tokens are part of decoding</h4>
<p>They are not passive metadata.</p>
<p>Changing timestamp generation changes sequence generation and segmentation behavior.</p>
<h4>6. Segmentation density matters</h4>
<p>Sparse segmentation can absorb short speech into neighboring dialogue.</p>
<p>Dense segmentation can give that same utterance its own decoding opportunity.</p>
<h4>7. The VAD chunk</h4>
</section>
